      "problem": "$\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{({sin}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$",
