      "problem": "${cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,({sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,({sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x) & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,({sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x)=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$",
